title: Folding in idle time
tags: Storage
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: What writes to many records leave pending is folded into the store by a thread of the repository, after thirty seconds without activity, one list per step, the biggest first.

A write to many records leaves lists pending — the entries of a part, the events of a
field, the trigrams kept by metarecord ([[Mass writes in the store]],
[[Text search in the store]]) — and every read pays for them until they are folded. The fold is work
the user did not ask for at that moment, so it waits for a moment when nobody is
asking for anything.

!! When

Each loaded repository has a thread (`folding.rs`, started with the hash worker). It
folds when the repository has been quiet for 30 seconds (`IDLE`). ''Activity'' is:

* ''every request to the repository'' — for as long as it lasts: a request that takes
  a minute does not end inside a quiet period, the 30 seconds count from its end. A
  client that polls, or a script that watches, says its requests are not activity with
  the header `X-Metafolder-Activity: off` (for `mf`, `METAFOLDER_ACTIVITY=off`); the
  GUI sets it on its change-feed poll;
* ''the user's actions the GUI reports'' ([[POST /repos/:repo/activity]]): the GUI
  (`lib/userActivity.ts`) reports each key, click and scroll, at most once every two
  seconds, to the repositories of the workspaces on screen;
* the load.

The watcher's writes and the daemon's own workers are not requests and do not count:
a file touched every few seconds must not keep the repository busy for ever.

!! How

One ''list'' per step — a part's entries, a field's events, a field's pending
trigrams — the biggest first (bytes for the first two, ids for the third), each in a
transaction of its own (`KvStore::fold_step`): a read or a write that arrives
waits for one list, not for all of them. After a fold the next step is one second
later (`NEXT`) if the repository is still quiet; with nothing left, the thread looks
again when it could be idle. No chunk size to tune: a list is the unit.

A deletion of many records waiting for its fold ([[Mass operations in the log]]) comes
before every other list, whatever the sizes: its records are read with the entries of
their parts still applied.

A step is not made while a coordinated revert holds the repository or the root is
absent. `mf repo fold` folds at once what is pending ([[POST /repos/:repo/fold]]), and `mf repo reindex` folds everything. A list has ''no limit'': a
write never folds one, so that a fold cannot start while someone works; how long the
lists have grown is the wear ([[Storage wear]]).

!! What is not here

The ''before'' of a mass replacement is still computed when the write is made, from the
field's indexes ([[Mass operations in the log]]), not at the fold.
